    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 70 tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` field on the owner, returned by `POST /api/owners` and `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nThe flag is `true` when **any** of the three signals hold, else `false`:\n\n1. **Possible duplicate** \u2014 the owner's persisted `possibleDuplicate` is true (existing soft-match on lastName + postcode).\n2. **Disposable-adjacent email** \u2014 the email domain shares a second-level name with a known disposable-email provider (`mailinator`, `tempmail`, `guerrillamail`) \u2014 catching variants that slip past the existing hard blocklist, such as a different TLD (`mailinator.net`) or a subdomain (`mail.mailinator.com`). Exact blocklisted domains are already rejected with 400 upstream.\n3. **City over soft capacity** \u2014 the persisted `capacityWarning` is true (city at/past the soft threshold of 40, below the hard cap of 50).\n\nChanges made:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `riskFlag` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n- **`OwnerRisk.java`** (new) \u2014 static helper computing the flag and the disposable-adjacency check, following the existing `OwnerSegments`/`AgeBands` helper pattern.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a MapStruct `@Mapping` expression wiring `riskFlag` to `OwnerRisk.flag(owner)`.\n\nBecause all three inputs are read from the entity (persisted `possibleDuplicate`/`capacityWarning`, plus the stored `email`), the flag is computed identically on create and on reload via GET \u2014 which is why `coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` (a normal owner with no email, no duplicate, an empty city) correctly reads `false`.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec's phrase \"disposable-adjacent\" wasn't defined elsewhere in the repo, so I defined it as a second-level-name match against the existing disposable blocklist (documented in `OwnerRisk` and the schema). If the intended definition differs (e.g. Levenshtein distance to blocklisted domains), the rule lives in one place and is easy to adjust.",
